; The scenario's properties re-asked as derivations, for `modality-cross-check.sh`
; — two independent implementations of one question, so a disagreement is a bug
; in one of them rather than a number to adjust.
;
; POLARITY, which every file here carries a warning about because it inverts
; silently:
;
;   possible    the SATISFYING set     non-empty ⟺ holds
;   never       the FORBIDDEN set      empty     ⟺ holds
;   live        the COUNTEREXAMPLE set empty     ⟺ holds
;   inevitable  the ESCAPE set         empty     ⟺ holds
;
; `must-decide-if-applied` is deliberately absent. It carries a `(fair …)`
; clause, and ct.rules §8 does not read one — it encodes the plain modality,
; over every run there is. Deriving it here would answer a different question
; from the one `writ check` answers and call the disagreement a bug. The
; cross-check skips a fair property for the same reason.

(load "stdlib.writ")
(load "ct.rules")

; possible — the satisfying set is the answer.
(satisfies can-commit (all (p participant) (is p.state committed)))

; never — the satisfying set of the FORBIDDEN guard, which must be empty.
(satisfies atomic (and (some (p participant) (is p.state committed))
                       (some (q participant) (is q.state aborted))))

; live — §7's three lines, ending in the counterexample set.
(satisfies decided (all (p participant)
  (or (is p.state committed) (is p.state aborted))))
(can-reach can-still-decide decided)
(trapped   can-decide       can-still-decide)

; inevitable — §8's three, ending in the escape set: the situations at which a
; run stops short of deciding, or starts going round without ever deciding.
(off-set  undecided decided)
(stays    waiting   undecided)
(escaping must-decide undecided waiting)
